---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Bench from '../../components/Bench.astro';
import LabelReader from '../../components/demos/LabelReader.astro';
import HoursToTimes from '../../components/demos/HoursToTimes.astro';
import { sortlogShots } from '../../data/sortlog-shots';

const spec = [
  { label: 'For', value: 'My crew at IQ Automotive Services' },
  { label: 'My role', value: 'Built it, and use it every shift to write the reports' },
  { label: 'When', value: 'September to October 2026' },
  { label: 'Used by', value: 'Site supervisors at the end of a shift; IQ’s office for reports and hours' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Used every shift since late September 2026' },
  { label: 'Built with', value: 'Next.js 16, Supabase, zxing-wasm, Claude Haiku, ExcelJS, jsPDF, Vitest' },
];

const shotFiles = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>('../../assets/sortlog/*.{png,jpg,jpeg,webp}', { eager: true });
const shots = sortlogShots
  .map((s) => ({ ...s, src: shotFiles[`../../assets/sortlog/${s.file}`]?.default }))
  .filter((s) => s.src);
---

<Case
  title="Sortlog: sorting reports by Navaneeth Krishnan"
  name="Sortlog"
  summary="Daily sorting reports and hours for a containment-sorting crew at IQ Automotive Services."
  description="How I built Sortlog: skid label photos in, IQ’s sorting reports and hours out, used every shift on the floor."
  spec={spec}
  next={{ href: '/work/amplifier', name: 'Amplifier' }}
>
  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The floor</h2>
      <p>
        When a supplier’s parts are suspect, a containment crew sorts every skid at the customer’s plant before the
        parts go on the line. We check each part, tag the rejects, and at the end of every shift report what was
        sorted, what was rejected, and who worked how long.
      </p>
      <p>
        That reporting was Excel. One workbook per part, filled in by hand from the skid labels and paper notes.
        Hours typed again into a daily hours sheet, then again into a monthly one. Every number lived in more than
        one place.
      </p>
      <p>
        I work on this floor and I write these reports, so I built the tool I wanted at the end of my own shift.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Bench title="Read a skid label" id="label-demo">
        <p slot="intro">
          At the end of a shift you photograph the labels of the skids you finished. The app reads every barcode,
          works out which value is which, and files the skid under its part. Here’s a made-up label.
        </p>
        <LabelReader />
      </Bench>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What I built</h2>
      <p>The end of a shift is one page, top to bottom.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Who worked,</strong> with everyone’s start and end time.</li>
        <li><strong>Skids.</strong> Label photos in, skids filed under their part. For each one: hours, suspect and missing counts, the reject tag, defect photos, and who sorted it. A skid that takes two days is picked up again the next morning.</li>
        <li><strong>Times,</strong> worked out per person and part from the skids, and editable.</li>
        <li><strong>Reports,</strong> in IQ’s own format: a sorting report per customer group and per part as PDF and Excel, daily and monthly hours, or all of them as one zip.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Around that: a reports page that answers “every part we’ve done for this customer and the total rejects”
        for any date range; a skid board built from the customer’s skid list, oldest first, so shipping can be asked
        for skids in order; and a company with sites, admins, managers and supervisors, where each company only
        ever sees its own data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Bench title="From skid hours to everyone’s times" id="times-demo">
        <p slot="intro">
          Nobody on the floor writes down when they started each part. They know how long a skid took and who was
          on it. Change the hours or the crew and watch the day lay out. Push the bracket skid to 8 hours, then end
          Lin’s shift at 9:00, and add Asha to the bracket skid.
        </p>
        <HoursToTimes />
      </Bench>
    </div>
  </section>

  {shots.length > 0 && (
    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem">The app</h2>
        <div class="shots shots-2">
          {shots.map((s) => (
            <figure>
              <Image src={s.src!} alt={s.alt} widths={[640, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 34rem, 100vw" />
              <figcaption>{s.caption}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Decisions</h2>
      <div class="decisions" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="decision">
          <h3>Barcodes first, Claude second</h3>
          <p>Barcodes are read in the browser, free and instant. Only when a label is torn or blurred does the photo go to Claude Haiku, which is asked for JSON and told to leave a field empty rather than guess a digit.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision">
          <h3>Point out problems, never fix them</h3>
          <p>Hours are pay. The app never moves time between people or parts. It splits a skid evenly, keeps everyone inside their shift, and flags what doesn’t add up: past an end time, two parts at once, hours nobody has room for.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision">
          <h3>Excel stays Excel</h3>
          <p>The office lives in spreadsheets, so the reports do too: IQ’s layout and colours, with live formulas for good parts, totals and suspect rates. Correct one number and everything that depends on it follows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision">
          <h3>Built for more than one site</h3>
          <p>Companies, sites and roles are separate in the database, with row-level security on every table. Adding a customer plant is a new site, not a new copy of the app.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem">Where it is now</h2>
      <dl class="outcome">
        <div class="field"><dt>Status</dt><dd>Used every shift since late September 2026</dd></div>
        <div class="field"><dt>Replaced</dt><dd>A workbook per part, a daily hours sheet and a monthly one</dd></div>
        <div class="field"><dt>Ready for</dt><dd>More sites, each with its own supervisor and crew</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</Case>
